  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "The sixth term of the geometric series is $2\\,187$ and the seventh term is $6\\,561.$ What is the sum of the first $8$ terms?\n\n${S}_{8}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio of the consecutive terms ${a}_{6}$ and ${a}_{7}:$\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}}=\\frac{6\\,561}{2\\,187}=3\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the value of ${a}_{1}.$ To do this, we use the formula for the $n$th term, which states that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{6-1} \\\\ 2\\,187 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ 2\\,187 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot 243 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=9,$ $N=8,$ and $r=3$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{8} & =9\\cdot \\frac{1-{3}^{8}}{1-3} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{1-{3}^{8}}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\cdot ({3}^{8}-1) \\\\  & =29\\,520.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
